<article class="env-definition" id="8244B3">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/8244B3">Definition <span data-tag="8244B3">6.4.2.3</span>.</a> Let $\calU $ be a partially ordered set. We will say that $\calU $ is a <em>locale</em> if the following conditions are satisfied:locale </p>
  <p><ul>
  <li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(1)$"><p>
Every subset $\{  U_{\alpha } \} $ of elements of $\calU $ has a least upper bound $\bigcup _{\alpha } U_{\alpha }$ in $\calU $. 
</p>
</li><li class="custom-item" data-marker="$(2)$"><p>
The formation of least upper bounds commutes with meets, in the sense that 
</p>
<div class="equation">
  \[  \bigcup (U_{\alpha } \cap V) = ( \bigcup U_{\alpha } ) \cap V. \]
</div>
<p>
 (Here $(U \cap V)$ denotes the greatest lower bound of $U$ and $V$, which exists in virtue of assumption $(1)$.) 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>